INT. CAROUSEL WORKSHOP - NIGHT

A cavernous barn of splintered painted horses, hanging by chains from the rafters, their glass eyes catching a single lantern. Sawdust drifts like snow.

MINA, twenties, a documentary photographer, raises a camera. TEDDY, thirties, her guide, grips a crowbar. OSRIC, sixties, the caretaker, stands rigid in the doorway with a ring of keys. LOLA, ten, Osric's granddaughter, clutches a plush rabbit.

MINA
Teddy, did you hear that?

TEDDY
It's the wind.

OSRIC
No wind in here.

LOLA
Grandpa, the horses are looking at me.

TEDDY
Lola, honey, they're wood.

MINA
Teddy, look up.

OSRIC
Lola, come here.

A chain clinks. One painted horse swings, slow. Another turns its head.

TEDDY
Mina, back up.

MINA
I'm trying.

LOLA
Grandpa!

The lantern gutters. All the horses begin to descend at once on their chains, slamming into the floor, shrieking, wheels turning, the whole carousel lurching into motion in the dark.
